<h1>Martin Luther and His Reform</h1>

<div class="header-info">
<p><strong>Martin Luther</strong> – Born November 10, 1483 in Eisleben, Saxony.  
Died February 18, 1546 in Eisleben, Saxony.</p>

<p>Married to Katharina von Bora from June 13, 1525 until his death.</p>

<p>His influence on the Protestant Reformation can be shown through three important events:  
The posting of the Theses, the Diet of Worms, and the translation of the Bible into German.</p>

<p><strong>02/26/2026</strong><br>
Chase Chriswisser, Tristan Dutil, Joel Fernandez</p>
</div>


<h2>Key Events in Luther's Reform</h2>

<div class="section">
<h3>The Posting of the 95 Theses (1517)</h3>
<p>
On October 31, Luther posted his 95 Theses on the door of a church in Wittenberg.  
These theses challenged the sale of indulgences and the papal authority in charge of the church.  
This is seen as the first step of the Protestant Reformation.
</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h3>The Diet of Worms (1521)</h3>
<p>
At this event, Luther was ordered to revoke his teachings before Emperor Charles V, but he refused.  
The Church then excommunicated him and this solidified the break between the churches.
</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h3>The German Bible Translation (1522-1534)</h3>
<p>
While in hiding, Luther translated the New Testament into German.  
This made Scripture available to the common public and supported his belief that the Bible is the ultimate authority in Christianity.
</p>
</div>



<h2>Disagreements</h2>

<div class="section">
<h3>1. Sale of Indulgences</h3>
<p>
Martin Luther strongly disagreed with the sale of indulgences, which were being sold by the Church as a way to reduce time in purgatory for oneself or loved ones.  
He believed that forgiveness could not be bought and that salvation comes only through God’s grace and faith.
</p>

<p class="quote">
“The Pope has no power to remit sins except by declaring and showing that they are remitted by God’s grace” (Luther, 1517).
</p>

<p>
He thought indulgences exploited the poor and distracted people from true repentance.  
For Luther, faith and Scripture were the ultimate authority.
</p>

<p>
The Catholic Church taught that indulgences were a legitimate way to reduce punishment for sins and that the Pope had the authority to grant them after confession and penance.
</p>
</div>


<div class="section">
<h3>2. Authority of Scripture versus Church Tradition</h3>

<p>
Luther believed that the Bible alone, rather than Church traditions or papal authority, should guide Christian belief and practice.
</p>

<p class="quote">
“It is neither right nor safe to depart from Scripture in matters of faith” (Luther, 1520).
</p>

<p>
He translated the Bible into the local language so all Christians could read and interpret it themselves.
</p>

<p>
The Catholic Church taught that both Scripture and Church Tradition were equally authoritative.  
Church councils, papal decrees, and established rituals were considered necessary for understanding God’s Word correctly.
</p>
</div>


<div class="section">
<h3>3. The Sacrament of Communion</h3>

<p>
Luther disagreed with the Catholic teaching of transubstantiation, which stated that the bread and wine literally become the body and blood of Christ.
</p>

<p>
He believed Christ is truly present in communion, but the bread and wine remain physically unchanged, a view called sacramental union.
</p>

<p class="quote">
“The body and blood of Christ are truly present and distributed to those who eat in faith” (Luther, 1520).
</p>

<p>
The Catholic Church taught that during Mass the bread and wine are fully transformed into Christ’s body and blood and that receiving the Eucharist is necessary for salvation.
</p>

</div>



